---
import JsonExample from './JsonExample.astro';
import { formatterFaqs, diffFaqs } from '../lib/json-examples.ts';
import { exactFormatterExample } from '../lib/json-examples.ts';
interface Props { tool: 'formatter' | 'diff'; }
const { tool } = Astro.props;
const faqs = tool === 'formatter' ? formatterFaqs : diffFaqs;
---
<section class="json-copy mt-7 space-y-5 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300" aria-label={tool === 'formatter' ? 'JSON formatting reference and examples' : 'JSON comparison reference and examples'}>
  <p class="rounded-lg border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50 p-3 dark:border-indigo-800 dark:bg-indigo-950/30">Need to check an API contract? <a href="/json-schema-validator/">JSON Schema Doctor</a> validates required fields, types, enums, and nested rules. <a href="/guides/validate-json-against-schema/">Try a four-error example →</a></p>
  {tool === 'formatter' ? <>
    <div>
      <h2>Format, validate, and explore JSON in your browser</h2>
      <p>A minified API response hides the relationship between fields. This free online JSON formatter adds two-space, four-space, or tab indentation so you can inspect objects and arrays. Use it as a JSON beautifier, pretty printer, syntax validator, or collapsible JSON viewer. Start with <strong>Load sample</strong>, paste a response, or open a local file.</p>
      <p><strong>Prettify</strong> formats valid JSON. <strong>Compact</strong> minifies it to one line. <strong>Validate</strong> checks syntax without changing your input. <strong>Undo</strong> restores the previous text. Copy or download the JSON, then use <strong>Share JSON</strong> to give someone a reproducible example. Formatting runs in a local worker with a Cancel action.</p>
    </div>
    <div><h2>JSON pretty-print example: inspect an API response</h2><JsonExample kind="formatter" /><p>The order is easier to follow after formatting: <code>orders</code> is an array, its first item contains <code>status</code> and <code>items</code>, and <code>pagination.hasNext</code> is a boolean. Formatting does not turn <code>false</code> into a string or remove spaces inside a string value.</p></div>
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div><h2>Find a nested value with the JSON tree</h2><p>Open Tree &amp; Fold, expand an object or array, and select a value. The selected-value panel copies a jq accessor such as <code>.orders[0].status</code>, a JSON Pointer such as <code>/orders/0/status</code>, or the exact value. Array indexes start at zero. <code>.["content.type"]</code> identifies one key, whereas <code>.content.type</code> follows two nested keys.</p><p>Branches load 50 items at a time, up to 1,500 displayed values. For remaining data, use Find in text. The tree copies paths; it does not run jq queries. See the <a href="/guides/format-validate-json-api-response/">JSON formatting and jq path guide</a>.</p></div>
      <div><h2>Fix common invalid JSON errors</h2><ul><li>Use double quotes for keys and strings: <code>{'{"name":"Ada"}'}</code>.</li><li>Remove trailing commas before <code>&#125;</code> or <code>]</code>.</li><li>Use lowercase <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, and <code>null</code>.</li><li>Escape quotes and line breaks inside strings.</li><li>Remove JavaScript comments and values such as <code>undefined</code> or <code>NaN</code>.</li></ul><p>The validator reports a line and column with a helpful syntax message. Jump to error selects the location. It does not silently repair or rewrite invalid input.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div><h2>Syntax validation, schema validation, and exact data</h2><p>Valid syntax does not prove that an API accepts a document. Required fields and allowed values need an API contract or <a href="/json-schema-validator/">JSON Schema Doctor</a>. Several objects on separate lines are <a href="/jsonl-viewer/">JSON Lines</a>, not one document.</p><p>Formatting copies original tokens rather than serializing JavaScript numbers. <code>9007199254740993</code>, <code>10.50</code>, <code>1E2</code>, string escapes, and every duplicate key survive unchanged. Other parsers may round an ID or keep only the last duplicate value. <a href={exactFormatterExample}>Try the exact-number example ↗</a> and read <a href="/guides/json-large-integers-duplicate-keys/">why JSON numbers change and duplicate keys disappear</a>. See <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259">RFC 8259</a> for interoperability rules.</p><p>Open files up to 10 MiB; nesting is bounded at 128 levels and formatted output at 20 MiB. Large inputs switch to text-only display for responsive scrolling. The complete text remains searchable, copyable, and downloadable.</p></div>
    <div><h2>Share a JSON debugging example</h2><p>Formatting happens locally without uploading pasted JSON. To share, select Share JSON and choose either an embedded-data URL or a stored short link. The embedded URL includes the data in its fragment; the short-link option explicitly stores it for 30 days. Anyone with either link can read the example, so replace secrets and personal values first.</p><p>For before-and-after payloads, use the <a href="/json-diff/">JSON diff checker</a>. To move records into a spreadsheet, use <a href="/csv-json-converter/">JSON to CSV</a>; for configuration formats, try <a href="/yaml-json-converter/">JSON to YAML</a>. If the payload begins with <code>H4sI</code>, follow the <a href="/guides/decode-base64-gzip-to-json/">Base64 GZip to JSON guide</a> before formatting it.</p></div>
  </> : <>
    <div><h2>Compare JSON by structure, values, and paths</h2><p>A text diff may show an entire response as changed because indentation or key order changed. This JSON diff checker parses both documents and compares their values. It reports added, removed, and modified properties, including changes inside nested objects and arrays. Use it to compare API responses, review a JSON configuration change, or investigate a webhook payload.</p><p>Paste the two documents, choose how to handle ordering, and select <strong>Compare JSON</strong>. The highlighted views show each version, while the Changes Report names the path and its old or new value. <strong>Swap</strong> reverses the comparison, so additions become removals.</p></div>
    <div><h2>JSON comparison example: configuration and reordered arrays</h2><JsonExample kind="diff" /><p>With default settings, <code>service.timeout</code> changes from <code>15</code> to <code>30</code>, <code>retries</code> is added, and the two region positions change. Enable Ignore array order and the region list matches, leaving two differences. Both links open the example with the relevant comparison settings.</p></div>
    <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div><h2>Ignore JSON key order</h2><p>Object members are unordered in the <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259">JSON specification</a>. With Ignore key order enabled, <code>{'{"a":1,"b":2}'}</code> and <code>{'{"b":2,"a":1}'}</code> compare as equal. Whitespace never changes the structural result. Disable the option if parsed property order is relevant to your review; use <a href="/text-diff/">Text Diff</a> when exact lines, indentation, or numeric spelling matter.</p></div>
      <div><h2>Compare JSON arrays with or without order</h2><p>Arrays compare by index by default: <code>[1,2]</code> differs from <code>[2,1]</code>. Choose Ignore array order for collections where positions are irrelevant, such as supported regions. Nested objects match by their values and duplicate counts are preserved: <code>[1,1,2]</code> still differs from <code>[1,2,2]</code>. The setting applies to every array, including nested ones; unmatched items appear as additions and removals, without joining records by ID. Keep order enabled for rankings, coordinates, and event sequences.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div><h2>Read meaningful changes, including null and type differences</h2><p>A missing field differs from a present field set to <code>null</code>. Adding <code>email: null</code> is an addition; changing an existing email to null is a modification. The number <code>1</code> differs from the string <code>"1"</code>, and an object differs from an array even if they look similar. Paths such as <code>orders[0].status</code> tell you where to investigate.</p><p>Each document must be valid JSON. If one fails to parse, the error identifies its input panel; use the <a href="/json-formatter/">JSON formatter and validator</a> to inspect it. JavaScript parsing retains the last duplicate object key and can round very large integers, so quote long identifiers and avoid duplicate property names.</p></div>
    <div><h2>Share a reproducible API response comparison</h2><p>Select <strong>Share comparison</strong> to include both documents and the ordering options. A reader can reopen the comparison instead of reconstructing it from a screenshot. The embedded-data link carries the example in its URL fragment; optional short links store it for 30 days. Replace tokens, customer details, and private values before sharing.</p><p>For a focused debugging workflow, read <a href="/guides/compare-json-api-responses-ignore-array-order/">how to compare API responses and handle array order</a>. When the input comes from a request, the <a href="/api-client/">REST API client</a> can help capture a response; the <a href="/guides/format-validate-json-api-response/">JSON formatting guide</a> explains syntax errors and copied tree paths.</p></div>
  </>}
  <div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="mt-3 space-y-2">{faqs.map(faq => <details class="rounded-lg border border-slate-200 bg-white dark:border-slate-700 dark:bg-slate-800"><summary class="cursor-pointer p-3 font-semibold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-100">{faq.question}</summary><p class="px-3 pb-3">{faq.answer}</p></details>)}</div></div>
</section>
<style>
  .json-copy { line-height: 1.8; }
  .json-copy h2 { margin-bottom: .5rem; color: #1e293b; font-size: 1.15rem; line-height: 1.45; font-weight: 650; }
  .json-copy p + p { margin-top: .65rem; }
  .json-copy code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; border-radius: .25rem; background: #f1f5f9; padding: .12rem .25rem; font-size: .88em; }
  .json-copy a:not([class]) { color: #4f46e5; text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .json-copy ul { list-style: disc; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  :global(.dark) .json-copy h2 { color: #f1f5f9; }
  :global(.dark) .json-copy code { background: #0f172a; }
  :global(.dark) .json-copy a:not([class]) { color: #a5b4fc; }
</style>
